Renditions per origin: a log-spaced ladder {362, 512, 724, 1024, 1448, 2048}
truncated at the source's long edge (UPSCALING IS REFUSED — the renderer
asserts, it does not silently upscale, because a synthetic upscale has no
high-frequency detail and would mislead every feature the gate conditions
on), then the smallest / middle / largest legal size, each materialized BOTH
as a centre `crop` (native detail statistics preserved) and as a `resize`
(CatmullRom = Mitchell-Netravali B=0 C=0.5, balanced blur/ringing). 310
renditions: 154 crop / 155 resize (one origin yields a duplicate at its
native size), 107 < 0.25 MP, 81 0.25-1 MP, 75 1-2 MP, 46 >= 2 MP.


================================================================ WHAT SHIPS

The sectioned local-tree writer used to gather all 14 candidate predictors
per group and then prune to a FIXED `SECTIONED_PRUNE_PREDICTORS_K = 8` by
ROOT COST. Two things are wrong with that and the corpus shows both:

 1. Root-cost ranking is the wrong selector — already refuted for the GLOBAL
    tree in `jxl_probe_prune_2026-08-15.md` (predictor value is
    leaf-conditional; screen trees put real mass on `Zero`, whose root cost
    is 15.9x worse than `Gradient`'s). Measured cost of that here: the
    SHIPPING K=8 default costs **+0.38 % bytes mean / +6.54 % max** vs no
    pruning at all on the train split, **+1.12 % mean** on the gb82-sc
    screens. That is a pre-existing regression nobody had measured, because
    the 2026-08-28 sweep used K=8 as its own baseline.
 2. Pruning AFTER the gather only shrinks the split search. Selecting the
    set BEFORE the gather shrinks the tokenization and the token/ebit columns
    too.

What ships instead: ONE whole-image probe tree per encode, and every group's
learn gets the predictors that tree's leaves actually use. The COUNT is
therefore chosen by content — 4 on a concentrated photo tree, 8 on a spread
document tree — which is the "content-adaptive K" the task asked for.

Three constants, all in `modular/section.rs`, two of them fitted here:

  SECTIONED_PROBE_MAX_NODES  = 2047   (measured, see below)
  SECTIONED_PROBE_MIN_LEAVES = 768    (fitted)
  SECTIONED_PROBE_COVERAGE_PCT = 80   (fitted)

plus the probe density `(group_stride, pixel_mult) = (1, 16)` at e7 and
`(1, 8)` at e8+. Env dials for all of them:
`JXL_SECTIONED_TREE_PREDICTORS` (auto|off|K),
`JXL_SECTIONED_PROBE_{MAX_NODES,MIN_LEAVES,COVERAGE,GROUP_STRIDE,PIXEL_MULT}`.


================================================================ WHAT FAILED

Recorded so the next session does not re-derive them.

* **Probing every 4th group (the global probe's density) is wrong here.**
  The global probe feeds ONE whole-image tree, so a group-level sample is
  unbiased for it. The sectioned probe feeds N per-group trees, and the whole
  point of sectioned mode is that groups differ. At group_stride 4 the worst
  byte cell was **+12.4 %** vs K=8 (a 4-group screenshot crop); covering
  EVERY group at the same total sample count (pixel stride 16 instead of 4)
  removed that failure mode. Group stride is now 1.

* **An uncapped probe tree eats its own saving.** On the 3840x2160 photo the
  probe grew a **9515-leaf** tree costing **1.39 s — 14 % of the whole
  encode**. Capping `max_property_values` (what the global probe does) does
  NOT bound tree size. With a node cap the cost flattens: 4095 nodes 748 ms,
  2047 nodes 706 ms, 1023 nodes 677 ms, 511 nodes 661 ms — the residue is the
  gather plus the top splits. 2047 sits on the plateau and keeps the most
  leaf resolution for the trust gate.

* **Masking the probe's property columns did nothing.** The global probe sets
  `active_props`; this one did not. Adding it moved the probe from 1403 ms to
  1384 ms. It is still the right thing (it is what the learn reads) and it
  stays, but it is not where the cost was.

* **Letting the adaptive set exceed 8 predictors is a wall regression.** On
  spread content the probe names 10-12 predictors. That buys bytes back, but
  it buys them with exactly the wall this work exists to remove: at t=8 the
  uncapped selector measured **+20 % wall** corpus-mean. The set is now hard
  capped at `SECTIONED_PRUNE_PREDICTORS_K`, so it can only ever be SMALLER
  than what ships today — and inside the cap the ranking is still the probe's
  leaf share rather than root cost, which is the better-founded selection at
  an equal count.

* **A sequential probe is Amdahl serial work.** Before the probe gather was
  parallelized, its share GREW with the thread count: ~+1 % of wall at t=1
  and ~+20 % at t=8. It now fans out across groups and merges in index order
  (deterministic — `append_from` is order-dependent and the order is fixed).

* **Small probe trees cannot be trusted.** On 362-px crops of charts and line
  art the probe learns 5-15 leaves, names 4-6 predictors, and the restriction
  costs up to **+8.3 % bytes**, because the real per-group learns (4x the
  samples, per group) reach for predictors the probe never split on. Those
  same cells have nothing to gain: dropping 14 -> 8 predictors moves their
  wall by under 5 %. Hence the trust gate. It is NOT a size gate — over the
  corpus, ZERO cells with 4 or fewer groups produce a trusted probe, so a
  separate `SECTIONED_PROBE_MIN_GROUPS` earns nothing and stays a no-op.


================================================================ THE FIT

Two parameters, fitted JOINTLY and evaluated offline from one sweep (a cell
with `leaves < min_leaves` contributes its K=8 row, which is exactly what the
gated encoder does, so no re-encode per candidate).

Selection rule, stated in `fit_sectioned_k_gate.py` BEFORE the numbers:
among candidates whose TRAIN worst-case per-rendition byte move vs K=8 is
<= 1.0 % and whose TRAIN mean byte move is <= 0, take the largest TRAIN mean
wall saving. Winner: **min_leaves = 768, coverage = 80 %**. Coverage 85 also
passes and is separated from 80 by 0.05 pp of corpus-mean t=8 wall — inside
run-to-run noise, and it produces the IDENTICAL predictor set on the bar
cell; a future re-fit with repeats may prefer it. Coverage 90 does not reach
the wall bar.

Gate firing rate: 49 of 310 renditions (16 %). It fires on photos, AI product
shots, colour patent rescans and one illustration. It NEVER fires on
documents (NOAA), web screenshots, plots/line-art, clipart, or the gb82-sc
screens — those are byte-identical to what ships today.

e7 t=8, full corpus (310 renditions), vs the shipping K=8 default:

  split       n    fires   bytes mean   median    p95     max   wall mean
  train      144      24      -0.001 %  +0.000  +0.136  +0.916    +1.85 %
  validate   138      25      +0.049 %  +0.000  +0.260  +2.991    +1.42 %
  continuity  28       0      +0.000 %  +0.000  +0.000  +0.000    +0.00 %

e7 t=1, >= 1 MP subset (122 renditions), vs the shipping K=8 default:

  split       n  fires |  ALL wall   ALL bytes | FIRED wall  median  FIRED bytes   max
  train      58     21 |   -5.81 %    -0.005 % |  -16.05 %  -16.56 %    -0.014 %  +0.916 %
  validate   56     20 |   -5.98 %    +0.112 % |  -16.73 %  -16.99 %    +0.314 %  +2.991 %
  continuity  8      0 |   +0.00 %    +0.000 % |   +0.00 %   +0.00 %    +0.000 %  +0.000 %
  ALL       122     41 |   -5.51 %    +0.049 % |  -16.38 %  -16.56 %    +0.146 %  +2.991 %

HELD-OUT READS HONESTLY ON WALL AND WORSE ON BYTES. The wall saving
generalises (-16.7 % held-out vs -16.1 % train); the byte tail does not
(+0.31 % mean / +2.99 % max held-out vs -0.01 % / +0.92 % train). The +2.99 %
is ONE cell — a colour patent rescan at 2048^2 — and it buys -20.3 % wall on
that same cell. It is present at every coverage below 100 and at every
min_leaves, so it is the probe getting that image's predictor set wrong, not
a threshold that can be nudged; coverage 100 avoids it and delivers no wall
win at all (-0.75 % on the fired cells).

Fired cells by size (e7 t=1) — the win grows with size, as the amortization
argument predicts:

  MP band     n   wall mean   median    best    worst   bytes mean    max
  1-2 MP     14    -15.01 %  -15.01 % -24.34 %  -5.77 %   +0.022 %  +0.948 %
  2-4 MP     13    -16.15 %  -15.69 % -27.15 %  -9.63 %   +0.105 %  +0.403 %
  >= 4 MP    14    -17.97 %  -18.99 % -22.96 %  -9.39 %   +0.307 %  +2.991 %

Reference arms on the SAME 41 fired cells (e7 t=1): no pruning (K=14) is
+33.12 % wall for +0.009 % bytes; the probe list with NO coverage cut
(coverage 100) is -0.75 % wall for -0.156 % bytes. The coverage cut is where
the wall comes from and where the bytes go.

Per content class, gate vs K=8 (all splits, e7 t=8; `fires` counts
renditions where the probe was trusted):

  class                                     n  fires  bytes mean  bytes max  wall mean
  1000-lilith-photos-general               30     15    +0.074 %   +0.948 %    +5.12 %
  1400-lilith-nature                       18     12    +0.038 %   +0.916 %    +1.21 %
  1600-lilith-food                          6      2    +0.150 %   +0.779 %    +2.61 %
